---
import TreatmentPage from "../../../components/TreatmentPage.astro";

const T = "/services/fertility-treatment";

const page = {
  slug: "fet",
  name: "Frozen Embryo Transfer",
  title: "Frozen Embryo Transfer (FET) in Lahore | Process",
  description:
    "Frozen embryo transfer (FET) in Lahore: how embryos are frozen and thawed, cycle types, preparation, success factors, risks and cost. Dr. Kamran Saleem.",
  eyebrow: "Frozen Embryo Transfer",
  h1: "Frozen Embryo Transfer (FET) in Lahore",
  waText: "Hello, I would like to book a frozen embryo transfer (FET) consultation with Dr. Kamran Saleem.",
  ctaLabel: "Book an FET consultation",
  heroSub: [
    "Frozen embryo transfer (FET) uses embryos that were frozen after an earlier IVF or ICSI cycle, thawing one and transferring it to the uterus in a carefully prepared cycle.",
    "At Kamran Fertility Clinic, Dr. Kamran Saleem plans FET cycles around your uterine lining, your embryo quality and your history, and explains why a frozen transfer may suit you better than a fresh one.",
  ].join(" "),
  leadIn: [
    { rich: ["Frozen transfers follow from an IVF cycle. If you are at an earlier stage, see our ", ["IVF treatment", `${T}/ivf`], " page first."] },
  ],
  sections: [
    {
      id: "what-is-fet",
      title: "What Is Frozen Embryo Transfer (FET)?",
      intro: "FET separates making embryos from transferring them, so the uterus can be prepared on its own schedule.",
      icon: "flask",
      body: [
        "In a fresh IVF cycle, an embryo is transferred a few days after egg retrieval. In a frozen embryo transfer, embryos are frozen after they develop, then thawed and transferred in a later cycle, usually one to several months afterwards.",
        "Modern freezing uses vitrification, a rapid process that turns embryos into a glass-like state without damaging ice crystals. Most good-quality embryos survive thawing.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "why-embryos-are-frozen",
      title: "Why Embryos Are Frozen",
      intro: "Freezing is a deliberate part of many treatment plans, not a sign that something went wrong.",
      icon: "user",
      body: [
        "Embryos may be frozen when:",
        { ul: [
          "There are spare good-quality embryos after a fresh transfer",
          "A high response to stimulation raises the risk of ovarian hyperstimulation, so transfer is delayed (a freeze-all strategy)",
          "The uterine lining is not ready, or hormone levels at the time of retrieval are unfavorable",
          "Embryos are being biopsied for genetic testing (PGT) and results are awaited",
          "A woman needs to postpone pregnancy for medical or personal reasons",
          "A transfer needs to be delayed for an illness or procedure",
        ] },
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "fet-cycle-types",
      title: "Types of FET Cycle",
      intro: "The best cycle type depends on whether you ovulate regularly and on your past results.",
      icon: "compare",
      body: [
        { h3: "Natural cycle FET" },
        "For women who ovulate regularly. Ovulation is tracked by ultrasound and blood tests, and transfer is timed to your own cycle with little or no medication.",
        { h3: "Modified natural cycle" },
        "As above, with a trigger injection to time ovulation exactly and progesterone support after transfer.",
        { h3: "Hormone replacement (programmed) cycle" },
        "Estrogen is given to build the lining, then progesterone is added when it is ready. It is convenient and predictable, and is used when ovulation is irregular.",
        "Some studies suggest a slightly higher risk of certain pregnancy complications, such as hypertensive disorders, in programmed cycles. Where suitable, Dr. Kamran may therefore prefer a natural or modified natural cycle.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "fet-step-by-step",
      title: "The FET Process, Step by Step",
      intro: "A frozen transfer cycle usually takes two to three weeks.",
      icon: "calendar",
      body: [
        { h3: "1. Review and planning" },
        "Your previous cycle, embryo grades, uterine cavity and any lining problems are reviewed, and the cycle type is chosen.",
        { h3: "2. Lining preparation and monitoring" },
        "Ultrasound scans check lining thickness and pattern, and sometimes hormone levels are measured, until the uterus is ready.",
        { h3: "3. Thawing and transfer" },
        "On the day, the embryo is thawed and its survival checked. It is then transferred with a thin catheter under ultrasound guidance. The procedure is brief and usually needs no anesthesia.",
        { h3: "4. Hormone support" },
        "Progesterone, and sometimes estrogen, continue after transfer until pregnancy is confirmed or ruled out.",
        { h3: "5. Pregnancy test" },
        "A blood test is usually done around 9 to 14 days after transfer, depending on the age of the embryo.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "fresh-vs-frozen-transfer",
      title: "Fresh vs Frozen Embryo Transfer",
      intro: "Neither is better for everyone.",
      icon: "refresh",
      body: [
        "Frozen transfer may be preferred when there is a risk of hyperstimulation, when PGT is used, when hormone levels at retrieval are unfavorable, or when the lining needs better preparation. A fresh transfer may be reasonable when the response to stimulation is moderate and the lining is receptive.",
        "A frozen transfer also lets you try again after a negative result without repeating stimulation and egg retrieval.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "fet-success-rates",
      title: "FET Success Rates: What Matters",
      intro: "The age at which the embryo was created matters more than how long it was frozen.",
      icon: "heart",
      body: [
        "The factors most likely to affect results are:",
        { ul: [
          "The woman's age when the eggs were collected",
          "Embryo quality, and whether it was frozen at the blastocyst stage",
          "Uterine receptivity, including lining thickness and any polyps, scarring or fibroids",
          "Underlying diagnosis, such as endometriosis",
          "Whether a single embryo is transferred, which reduces the risk of multiple pregnancy",
        ] },
        "Dr. Kamran will give you an honest estimate based on your embryos and history, not a headline figure.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "fet-risks",
      title: "FET Risks and Considerations",
      intro: "FET avoids repeat stimulation, but it is still a medical treatment.",
      icon: "shield",
      body: [
        { ul: [
          "Not every embryo survives thawing, though most good-quality ones do",
          "Estrogen and progesterone can cause bloating, headaches, mood changes and breast tenderness",
          "Multiple pregnancy if more than one embryo is transferred",
          "Ectopic pregnancy and miscarriage, as in any pregnancy",
          "Possible differences in pregnancy outcomes between cycle types, discussed above",
        ] },
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "fet-cost-and-storage",
      title: "FET Cost and Embryo Storage",
      intro: "FET is usually less costly than a fresh IVF cycle, since stimulation and egg retrieval are not repeated.",
      icon: "coin",
      body: [
        "Costs typically include the consultation, medication, monitoring scans, thawing and transfer, and embryo storage fees. Storage terms and consent forms are explained in writing, including what happens if storage lapses.",
        { rich: ["You will receive an itemized estimate during your consultation. For overall IVF costs, see ", ["IVF treatment cost in Pakistan", "/resources/ivf-treatment-cost-in-pakistan"], "."] },
      ],
    },
  ],
  faqs: [
    { q: "What is frozen embryo transfer?", a: "FET is a treatment in which embryos frozen after an earlier IVF or ICSI cycle are thawed and transferred to the uterus in a prepared cycle." },
    { q: "Is FET better than a fresh transfer?", a: "Not always. It is often preferred when there is a risk of hyperstimulation, when embryos are genetically tested, or when the lining needs more careful preparation." },
    { q: "How are embryos frozen and thawed?", a: "Embryos are frozen by vitrification, a very rapid process that avoids damaging ice crystals. They are thawed on the day of transfer and checked before being placed in the uterus." },
    { q: "How long can embryos stay frozen?", a: "Embryos can remain viable for many years. Storage periods and fees are set out in your consent form." },
    { q: "How long does a FET cycle take?", a: "Usually two to three weeks, including lining preparation, scans, transfer and hormone support, before the pregnancy test." },
    { q: "Is frozen embryo transfer painful?", a: "No. It is similar to a cervical smear and generally needs no anesthesia. Medication can cause mild side effects." },
    { q: "When do I take a pregnancy test after FET?", a: "A blood test is usually done about 9 to 14 days after transfer, depending on the age of the embryo." },
    { q: "What is the success rate of FET?", a: "It depends mainly on age at egg collection and embryo quality. Your doctor will give an individual estimate after reviewing your embryos." },
  ],
  closingHeading: "Frozen Embryo Transfer in Lahore With Dr. Kamran Saleem",
  closingParas: [
    "At Kamran Fertility Clinic, Dr. Kamran Saleem plans frozen embryo transfer cycles for women in Lahore, choosing the cycle type that suits your ovulation, lining and history.",
    "We review embryo records from other clinics too, so you do not need to repeat a full cycle when your frozen embryos can be used.",
  ],
  resources: [
    { href: "/resources/ivf-treatment-in-lahore", category: "Fertility Treatment", title: "IVF Treatment in Lahore: Cost, Process, Tests & Choosing the Right Clinic" },
    { href: "/resources/ivf-treatment-cost-in-pakistan", category: "Fertility Treatment", title: "IVF Treatment Cost in Pakistan 2026" },
  ],
  close: {
    heading: "Have Frozen Embryos?",
    body: "Bring your embryo freezing report and previous cycle summary to your consultation, so your FET plan builds on them.",
    cta: "Book an FET Consultation",
  },
};
---

<TreatmentPage {...page} />
